1289
00:56:36,103 --> 00:56:38,673
(The average price is about 17 dollars per person.)

1290
00:56:39,773 --> 00:56:42,403
(Almost double the other restaurants)

1291
00:56:42,773 --> 00:56:45,313
Jeonju's food trend changes very quickly.

1292
00:56:45,313 --> 00:56:49,483
They like sensational stuff, both young and old.

1293
00:56:50,153 --> 00:56:52,553
Jeonju is known for bibim dishes.

1294
00:56:52,983 --> 00:56:54,883
I really didn't want to do bibimbap.

1295
00:56:55,053 --> 00:56:56,083
Instead...

1296
00:56:56,083 --> 00:56:57,523
(Japanese, Chinese, Korean, Western)

1297
00:56:57,523 --> 00:57:00,093
Since I run Japanese, Chinese, Korean, and Western restaurants,

1298
00:57:00,963 --> 00:57:02,993
I'll mix them into the course menu.

1299
00:57:03,093 --> 00:57:04,163
(Western, Korean, Western, Western, Japanese, Korean, Western)

1300
00:57:04,163 --> 00:57:06,633
(Multinational course meal)

1301
00:57:06,633 --> 00:57:09,603
The course starts with beef tartare and menchi katsu.

1302
00:57:10,403 --> 00:57:12,903
Next is perilla oil makguksu...

1303
00:57:12,903 --> 00:57:14,643
and pork tenderloin steak.

1304
00:57:15,203 --> 00:57:17,043
Korean melon salad, bracken hangover soup,

1305
00:57:17,043 --> 00:57:19,043
and it ends with buckwheat crepe tiramisu.

1306
00:57:19,213 --> 00:57:20,283
However,

1307
00:57:21,343 --> 00:57:23,383
they do take a lot of effort to cook.

1308
00:57:23,383 --> 00:57:25,453
(They do take a lot of effort to cook.)

1309
00:57:26,353 --> 00:57:29,623
(For menchi katsu, they need to knead it 1 by 1.)

1310
00:57:30,053 --> 00:57:33,423
(Coat it in flour, egg, and breadcrumbs.)

1311
00:57:34,193 --> 00:57:36,763
(Deep-fry them 1 by 1.)

1312
00:57:37,293 --> 00:57:40,363
(Even the salad dressing is made from scratch using Korean melon.)

1313
00:57:40,863 --> 00:57:43,633
(Grilling the tenderloin piece by piece over charcoal)

1314
00:57:43,703 --> 00:57:45,373
(18 prep steps for meats and vegetables)

1315
00:57:45,373 --> 00:57:46,743
(Prepping cucumbers, Korean melons, cabbage, corn, beets, red onions)

1316
00:57:46,743 --> 00:57:48,073
(Chopping onions, prepping sorrel and perilla leaves, and such)

1317
00:57:48,073 --> 00:57:49,103
(10 sauces and bases)

1318
00:57:49,103 --> 00:57:50,173
(Making button mushroom paste, jalapeno mayo sauce, makguksu soup,)

1319
00:57:50,173 --> 00:57:51,173
(perilla oil sauce, perilla pesto, peanut butter sauce, and such)

1320
00:57:51,173 --> 00:57:52,813
(8 steps for cooking and desserts)

1321
00:57:52,813 --> 00:57:54,943
(Deep-frying menchi katsu, stripping meat from pork spine)

1322
00:57:54,943 --> 00:57:57,053
(Cooking buckwheat noodles, hangover soup, and such)

1323
00:57:57,113 --> 00:57:59,253
We chose bracken...

1324
00:57:59,253 --> 00:58:01,453
as the local specialty of Jeonju.

1325
00:58:01,453 --> 00:58:05,023
We are going to make bracken hangover soup using bracken.

1326
00:58:05,893 --> 00:58:09,693
(Boil the pork spine to pick off all the lean meat,)

1327
00:58:10,193 --> 00:58:13,503
(blanch the bracken,)

1328
00:58:13,763 --> 00:58:16,733
(blend it finely,)

1329
00:58:17,273 --> 00:58:20,643
(and stir it into the broth.)

1330
00:58:21,103 --> 00:58:24,813
(And bracken hangover soup is done.)

1331
00:58:24,813 --> 00:58:26,643
I tried to stick strictly to the book...

1332
00:58:26,643 --> 00:58:28,983
from prepping to cooking.

1333
00:58:29,383 --> 00:58:32,753
Even during the mission where I made tendon,

1334
00:58:32,753 --> 00:58:36,453
a lot of chefs asked me why I was doing things so by the book...

1335
00:58:36,453 --> 00:58:37,623
instead of hurrying it along.

1336
00:58:38,393 --> 00:58:39,993
- I have a question.
- Yes.

1337
00:58:39,993 --> 00:58:40,993
(And again, in the finals)

1338
00:58:40,993 --> 00:58:42,723
Do the dishes come out 1 by 1 as a course meal?

1339
00:58:43,363 --> 00:58:46,363
This time as well, they were like, "Will that even work?"

1340
00:58:46,803 --> 00:58:50,303
"Can a course meal really work when this is a competition?"

1341
00:58:50,303 --> 00:58:52,803
I thought, "Why are they taking unreasonable risks again?"

1342
00:58:53,233 --> 00:58:55,403
If the target audience is foreigners and Gen Z,

1343
00:58:55,673 --> 00:58:58,013
I think they would find 34 dollars burdensome.

1344
00:58:58,013 --> 00:59:00,813
If it were for families, I think they wouldn't eat it.

1345
00:59:01,443 --> 00:59:03,813
I don't think it fits any customer target audience.

1346
00:59:04,413 --> 00:59:07,023
Can it deliver 34 dollars' worth of satisfaction here?

1347
00:59:07,023 --> 00:59:08,453
(Satisfaction needs to be as high as the steep price point.)

1348
00:59:08,453 --> 00:59:12,023
We kind of took a gamble.

1349
00:59:12,023 --> 00:59:15,393
It might be all or nothing, but we chose a course meal.

1350
00:59:15,823 --> 00:59:18,393
It's all or nothing anyway, since it's the finals.

1351
00:59:18,533 --> 00:59:20,503
Those young guys are impressive.

1352
00:59:21,033 --> 00:59:22,933
We will prepare diligently,

1353
00:59:23,003 --> 00:59:25,373
and we'll try to pull it off steadily and quietly.

1354
00:59:25,373 --> 00:59:26,673
(Will their gamble end up all or nothing?)

1355
00:59:26,673 --> 00:59:28,303
(Son of Jeonju)

1356
00:59:30,473 --> 00:59:32,313
- Are you nervous, Hoon?
- No.

1357
00:59:32,843 --> 00:59:34,213
Don't be nervous.

1358
00:59:34,213 --> 00:59:35,383
(Seoul Jumbo Pork Cutlet and Hamburger Steak)

1359
00:59:35,443 --> 00:59:37,583
Does it show? No, I'm not nervous.

1360
00:59:38,113 --> 00:59:40,983
We kept labour costs the tightest.

1361
00:59:40,983 --> 00:59:43,283
Other teams used 2 part-timers each, but we used 1.

1362
00:59:43,493 --> 00:59:45,623
(200 dollars, 200 dollars)

1363
00:59:45,623 --> 00:59:48,093
(200 dollars, 200 dollars)

1364
00:59:48,093 --> 00:59:50,633
(200 dollars)

1365
00:59:50,663 --> 00:59:52,463
If we save just 200 dollars each,

1366
00:59:52,463 --> 00:59:54,363
we start out 400 dollars ahead.

1367
00:59:54,363 --> 00:59:55,403
I think we're going to win.

1368
00:59:55,403 --> 00:59:57,233
We have to, having come all this way.

1369
00:59:57,503 --> 00:59:58,873
It's not overconfidence.

1370
00:59:59,303 --> 01:00:02,273
One of my restaurants is about this scale.

1371
01:00:02,273 --> 01:00:04,543
If we run around really fast, three people can pull it off.

1372
01:00:04,843 --> 01:00:08,413
It's a game I've played 365 times a year for years,

1373
01:00:08,413 --> 01:00:09,543
so three people are more than enough.

1374
01:00:10,383 --> 01:00:11,513
- Let's do this.
- Let's go.

1375
01:00:11,513 --> 01:00:13,583
- Let's go.
- What we prepared this time is...

1376
01:00:13,583 --> 01:00:15,253
jumbo pork cutlet...

1377
01:00:15,553 --> 01:00:17,793
and hamburger steak with lasagna.

1378
01:00:17,793 --> 01:00:18,793
(Hamburger steak with lasagna, 17.90 dollars)

1379
01:00:18,793 --> 01:00:20,593
One, two, three. Gosh, what's that?

1380
01:00:21,893 --> 01:00:24,863
Isn't doing hamburger steak and pork cutlet...

1381
01:00:24,863 --> 01:00:26,433
too predictable?

1382
01:00:26,903 --> 01:00:28,403
I think Kim Hoon's team...

1383
01:00:28,403 --> 01:00:31,073
would not be picked much.

1384
01:00:31,733 --> 01:00:33,643
The menu was cliche.

1385
01:00:33,643 --> 01:00:34,703
(Chef's jumbo pork cutlet and hamburger steak with lasagna)

1386
01:00:34,703 --> 01:00:37,743
We've always cooked tailored to mass appeal, you know.

1387
01:00:37,943 --> 01:00:39,273
Tenderloin steak rice,

1388
01:00:39,273 --> 01:00:40,513
cream shrimp and sweet fried chicken,

1389
01:00:40,513 --> 01:00:41,813
prawn yakisoba,

1390
01:00:41,813 --> 01:00:42,983
jambon cream pasta,

1391
01:00:42,983 --> 01:00:44,313
lasagna cheese balls,

1392
01:00:44,313 --> 01:00:47,023
meat-packed kimchi stew, and spicy stir-fried pork.

1393
01:00:47,553 --> 01:00:49,653
We always make mass-appeal food.

1394
01:00:50,223 --> 01:00:53,893
This time, the theme is "Everyone's Restaurant," after all.

1395
01:00:53,893 --> 01:00:55,893
Pork cutlet and hamburger steak are dishes...

1396
01:00:55,893 --> 01:00:57,863
that have always been by our side.

1397
01:00:58,233 --> 01:01:00,333
I think we came up with the items with the least room for division.

1398
01:01:01,033 --> 01:01:05,033
(Competing in the finals with crowd-pleasing menus again)

1399
01:01:05,133 --> 01:01:07,903
(Hamburger steak sauce)

1400
01:01:07,903 --> 01:01:09,943
(However)

1401
01:01:09,943 --> 01:01:11,073
What is this burnt smell?

1402
01:01:11,973 --> 01:01:13,173
I smell something burning.

1403
01:01:15,443 --> 01:01:16,443
(What?)

1404
01:01:18,183 --> 01:01:19,283
(Sighing)

1405
01:01:27,223 --> 01:01:28,323
It's burnt.

1406
01:01:29,793 --> 01:01:32,733
I had a total mental breakdown back then.

1407
01:01:33,263 --> 01:01:34,663
This is the worst.

1408
01:01:36,833 --> 01:01:37,873
Let me taste it.

1409
01:01:47,443 --> 01:01:48,543
We can't use this.

1410
01:01:49,883 --> 01:01:51,113
- We can't use this.
- What do we do?

1411
01:01:51,483 --> 01:01:54,023
I tried it, and it had an unsalvageable burnt taste.

1412
01:01:54,023 --> 01:01:55,653
But we prepared...

1413
01:01:55,653 --> 01:01:57,653
just enough hamburger steak sauce for our use.

1414
01:01:58,093 --> 01:02:00,123
(Urgent measures need to be taken.)

1415
01:02:01,163 --> 01:02:03,393
(Teammate Kim Jong In assesses the situation.)

1416
01:02:03,393 --> 01:02:05,533
The thing that burned just now is the demi-glace sauce, right?

1417
01:02:05,993 --> 01:02:07,963
- Does the entire sauce taste burnt?
- Yes.

1418
01:02:09,363 --> 01:02:11,833
We have plenty of cream. Let's go with cream for the lasagna.

1419
01:02:12,833 --> 01:02:14,543
We have plenty of heavy cream and chilli oil for the lasagna.

1420
01:02:15,003 --> 01:02:16,943
Blend the shiitake mushrooms and put them in together.

1421
01:02:16,943 --> 01:02:20,113
There were certain ingredients we had plenty of left over.

1422
01:02:20,113 --> 01:02:21,213
What burned? Did the bottom burn?

1423
01:02:21,213 --> 01:02:22,813
- I think the bottom burnt.
- It's burnt.

1424
01:02:23,043 --> 01:02:24,213
If it smells, throw it out.

1425
01:02:24,613 --> 01:02:28,123
(Scraping the entire batch)

1426
01:02:28,483 --> 01:02:29,653
It's okay.

1427
01:02:30,423 --> 01:02:32,023
We needed to make a new sauce,

1428
01:02:32,023 --> 01:02:33,693
but we didn't have the ingredients.

1429
01:02:33,693 --> 01:02:35,393
We gathered all the heavy cream we had.

1430
01:02:36,693 --> 01:02:38,733
And I went to Team Son of Jeonju.

1431
01:02:38,733 --> 01:02:41,163
Could you spare and lend us any leftover minced garlic?

1432
01:02:41,433 --> 01:02:42,963
- We haven't started yet.
- Really?

1433
01:02:42,963 --> 01:02:44,703
- So we will give you three for now.
- Thank you.

1434
01:02:44,703 --> 01:02:45,733
- Good luck.
- Thank you.

1435
01:02:45,733 --> 01:02:47,303
I borrowed garlic from them.

1436
01:02:47,803 --> 01:02:50,173
I'm sorry, but if you have starch, could we get just a little?

1437
01:02:50,173 --> 01:02:51,213
Here you go.

1438
01:02:51,213 --> 01:02:52,413
We borrowed starch.

1439
01:02:52,413 --> 01:02:54,983
We had chilli oil, so we added spicy flavour with chilli oil.

1440
01:02:54,983 --> 01:02:57,383
We made a sauce using the ingredients on hand.

1441
01:02:57,383 --> 01:02:58,583
(A new hamburger steak sauce)

1442
01:02:58,583 --> 01:02:59,983
The sauce changed.

1443
01:03:00,053 --> 01:03:01,653
What we originally made was a demi-glace sauce,

1444
01:03:01,653 --> 01:03:03,323
but it changed to a cream mushroom sauce.

1445
01:03:04,023 --> 01:03:06,893
(Tasting the improvised sauce)

1446
01:03:06,893 --> 01:03:08,263
This is good. Well done.

1447
01:03:08,463 --> 01:03:09,963
- Is it over?
- It's done.

1448
01:03:09,963 --> 01:03:11,293
Let's make the lasagna together right away.

1449
01:03:12,463 --> 01:03:14,503
I brought out lasagna, which is my specialty.

1450
01:03:14,503 --> 01:03:17,773
I think that's the dish that made Kim Hoon who he is today.

1451
01:03:17,973 --> 01:03:20,803
(Lasagna sheet)

1452
01:03:20,803 --> 01:03:23,473
Layer lasagna,

1453
01:03:23,473 --> 01:03:25,343
rice, ragu sauce,

1454
01:03:25,343 --> 01:03:28,113
hamburger steak, and cream sauce...

1455
01:03:28,113 --> 01:03:30,853
in that order on a foil plate,

1456
01:03:30,853 --> 01:03:32,513
and bake for 7 to 10 minutes...

1457
01:03:32,513 --> 01:03:34,353
until the cheese turns golden brown.

1458
01:03:34,853 --> 01:03:37,253
And right from the start, when designing the menu,

1459
01:03:37,253 --> 01:03:40,053
we only composed it of dishes that could pick up speed.

1460
01:03:40,823 --> 01:03:43,193
Pork cutlet is cooked in a deep fryer,

1461
01:03:43,393 --> 01:03:46,693
and the soba is done once we boil the noodles and pour the broth over.

1462
01:03:46,693 --> 01:03:47,803
(They pour the broth and serve it right away.)

1463
01:03:47,803 --> 01:03:49,763
The hamburger steak is ready once we take it out of the oven.

1464
01:03:49,963 --> 01:03:53,173
We planned the dishes that Jong In could cook quickly.

1465
01:03:54,373 --> 01:03:55,403
It's 12:30pm now.

1466
01:03:55,403 --> 01:03:57,473
- I'll set up the dining hall.
- Go.

1467
01:03:58,113 --> 01:04:00,343
It's quite spacious.

1468
01:04:02,043 --> 01:04:05,653
Looking at other teams' menus and our price range,

1469
01:04:05,653 --> 01:04:07,783
I figured we had to compete on table turnover rate.

1470
01:04:07,783 --> 01:04:09,923
So I thought we should sweep up all the tables.

1471
01:04:10,383 --> 01:04:14,093
(Strategy: Increase tables to compete on turnover rate.)

1472
01:04:14,463 --> 01:04:17,533
First, I turned the 4-seater tables into 2-seater tables.

1473
01:04:17,533 --> 01:04:18,593
(Turning the 4-seater tables into 2-seater tables)

1474
01:04:18,963 --> 01:04:20,463
We were equally given 9 tables each,

1475
01:04:20,463 --> 01:04:21,663
but we separated them all.

1476
01:04:21,663 --> 01:04:23,733
Then we end up with about twice as many tables.

1477
01:04:23,733 --> 01:04:24,873
Five, six...

1478
01:04:26,673 --> 01:04:29,243
(Calculating something)

1479
01:04:31,043 --> 01:04:33,043
We need to buy them all.

1480
01:04:33,813 --> 01:04:35,383
We'll buy all six tables.

1481
01:04:35,843 --> 01:04:37,043
We'll buy everything you have.

1482
01:04:37,653 --> 01:04:40,553
(They bought extra tables for 120 dollars.)

1483
01:04:40,553 --> 01:04:42,053
Since we have three workers,

1484
01:04:42,053 --> 01:04:44,353
we're one person short compared to others.

1485
01:04:44,353 --> 01:04:45,993
Then when customers leave,

1486
01:04:45,993 --> 01:04:47,893
we might not be able to clean up immediately to seat the next ones,

1487
01:04:47,893 --> 01:04:49,493
so if we just have plenty of tables,

1488
01:04:49,493 --> 01:04:51,763
we can seat them at empty tables without having to clear them.

1489
01:04:51,763 --> 01:04:53,093
So we decided to lay out as many tables as possible.

1490
01:04:53,333 --> 01:04:57,833
(A turnover strategy taking on extra costs)

1491
01:05:05,343 --> 01:05:06,373
This is tough.

1492
01:05:07,383 --> 01:05:08,783
I bought up all the spare tables.

1493
01:05:08,783 --> 01:05:09,813
I got them all.

1494
01:05:10,383 --> 01:05:11,653
(Labour cost: Son of Jeonju, 400 dollars, Noodle Shop, 400 dollars,)

1495
01:05:11,653 --> 01:05:12,883
(Seoul Jumbo Pork Cutlet and Hamburger Steak, 200 dollars)

1496
01:05:12,883 --> 01:05:14,123
(Table: standard table, 40 dollars, bar table, 20 dollars)

1497
01:05:14,123 --> 01:05:15,723
(Son of Jeonju, 360 dollars, Noodle Shop, 460 dollars,)

1498
01:05:15,723 --> 01:05:17,053
(Seoul Jumbo Pork Cutlet and Hamburger Steak, 480 dollars)

1499
01:05:20,153 --> 01:05:21,763
Even while doing business in Seoul,

1500
01:05:21,763 --> 01:05:24,233
I always held pride in flavour...

1501
01:05:24,233 --> 01:05:25,833
as a chef from Jeonju.

1502
01:05:25,833 --> 01:05:27,163
(A course meal aiming for a high average spend per customer)

1503
01:05:27,163 --> 01:05:28,733
I wanted to put in...

1504
01:05:28,733 --> 01:05:30,903
all the know-how earned through blood and sweat...

1505
01:05:30,903 --> 01:05:33,773
to present dishes I could show with confidence.

1506
01:05:33,873 --> 01:05:36,703
No one in Korea who would dislike pork cutlet and hamburger steak.

1507
01:05:36,703 --> 01:05:38,673
They are dishes that have always been right by our side.

1508
01:05:38,673 --> 01:05:40,313
Without a doubt, we will take first place on this.

1509
01:05:41,143 --> 01:05:42,243
Let's do this.

1510
01:05:42,713 --> 01:05:44,883
This is the final. Let's have fun.

1511
01:05:45,053 --> 01:05:46,583
- Let's do this.
- Let's do this.

1512
01:05:46,753 --> 01:05:47,853
Let's try our best, Jong In.

1513
01:05:47,883 --> 01:05:48,953
Please.

1514
01:05:49,383 --> 01:05:51,523
- Tae Hee, prep our battle stations.
- Battle stations.

1515
01:05:51,523 --> 01:05:53,623
- This is the final.
- This is the final.

1516
01:05:53,753 --> 01:05:54,763
Let's go!

1517
01:05:59,663 --> 01:06:02,463
We will now begin the finals,

1518
01:06:03,633 --> 01:06:06,973
Everyone's Restaurant.

1519
01:06:06,973 --> 01:06:09,573
(Everyone's Restaurant is open.)

1520
01:06:09,743 --> 01:06:10,943
We are going to win.

1521
01:06:11,643 --> 01:06:12,713
We're almost there.

1522
01:06:13,413 --> 01:06:14,583
This is the beginning.

1523
01:06:14,743 --> 01:06:17,183
In the final round, different target demographics...

1524
01:06:17,183 --> 01:06:19,053
will visit across time slots.

1525
01:06:19,053 --> 01:06:20,453
(Final Round, Everyone's Restaurant)

1526
01:06:20,453 --> 01:06:22,053
(Different target demographics visiting by time slot)

1527
01:06:22,053 --> 01:06:23,583
(Seniors, office workers, students, foreigners, restaurateurs, families)

1528
01:06:23,623 --> 01:06:27,023
The first group is restaurateurs.

1529
01:06:27,023 --> 01:06:28,493
- Darn it.
- Right from the start?

1530
01:06:29,263 --> 01:06:30,393
I'm scared.

1531
01:06:30,663 --> 01:06:32,533
We're starting with restaurateurs.

1532
01:06:33,193 --> 01:06:35,203
Gosh, we're in trouble.

1533
01:06:35,503 --> 01:06:36,663
(Sighing)

1534
01:06:36,663 --> 01:06:37,873
Restaurateurs from the start?

1535
01:06:37,873 --> 01:06:38,873
